From: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: stop paying for flow counters the NPU never provides

Two separate ways the driver gives away offload capacity.

The first is the counter carve-out.  airoha_ppe_get_num_stats_entries()
answers from the build and the SoC alone, so every AN7581 reserves
PPE_STATS_NUM_ENTRIES worth of each PPE's SRAM for counters before anyone
has asked the NPU whether it can count at all.  On a board whose NPU
answers -EINVAL, airoha_ppe_offload_setup() sets foe_stats_enabled to
false and the warning says "continuing without counters" - but the SRAM is
still carved out.  Half of each PPE stays unusable for the life of the
boot to hold counters that were never handed to the NPU.  Keep the
question "could this build count" separate from "is it counting now": the
capability answer sizes the buffer that gets handed over, while the
geometry answer decides the carve-out, and only the second one has to look
at foe_stats_enabled.  Nothing allocates less because of this - the buffer
is still sized for the capability, and the geometry only shrinks.

The second is the DRAM half of the table.  The hardware has 16384 entries
there and the driver already programs everything they need: TB_BASE points
straight at the buffer, DRAM_TB_NUM_ENTRY holds shift(16384), and the hash
mode is set.  Only PPE_DRAM_TABLE_EN and, symmetrically with the SRAM
side's PPE_SRAM_HASH1_EN, PPE_DRAM_HASH1_EN are missing, so the enable
mask clears them and the hardware never looks at the region.  airoha_ppe_
get_total_num_entries() counts those 16384 entries anyway, which is what
makes it hurt: fold_hash() spreads hashes over [0, 32768) while
airoha_ppe_foe_commit_entry() only programs the ones below the SRAM total,
and the rest fall out of the function with err still 0.  The flow gets
marked HW_OFFLOAD and is then forwarded by the CPU.

Turning the table on makes those hashes land somewhere the hardware
actually searches.  No write path is needed: this table is host memory
that TB_BASE addresses directly, so the memcpy that commit_entry() already
does is the write, and dmam_alloc_coherent() has zeroed the region, which
BIND_STATE reads as AIROHA_FOE_STATE_INVALID - a miss, exactly what the
hardware does today.  The worst case of getting this wrong is therefore
the behaviour we already have, not a false hit.

Keep folding on the hash the hardware reported rather than re-deriving a
slot, so driver and hardware cannot disagree about which entry a flow
owns; the only remaining way to leave commit_entry() without programming
anything is a hash past the end of the table, which folding rules out, and
that now reports -EINVAL instead of claiming success.

A readback after the programming warns once per PPE if the bit did not
stick, because the failure is silent otherwise: the entries would simply
never match.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c	2026-10-21 17:26:20.380663285 +0800
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c	2026-10-21 18:09:52.912493219 +0800
@@ -38,7 +38,13 @@
 	.automatic_shrinking = true,
 };
 
-static int airoha_ppe_get_num_stats_entries(struct airoha_ppe *ppe)
+/* How many counter slots per PPE this build could ask the NPU for.  This is
+ * a property of the build and the SoC, not of what the NPU ended up agreeing
+ * to, and it has to stay that way: the counter buffer is allocated in
+ * airoha_ppe_init() and handed to the NPU in airoha_ppe_offload_setup(), both
+ * of which run before there is an answer to ask for.
+ */
+static int airoha_ppe_stats_capable_entries(struct airoha_ppe *ppe)
 {
 	if (!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_NET_AIROHA_FLOW_STATS))
 		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
@@ -49,9 +55,25 @@
 	return PPE_STATS_NUM_ENTRIES;
 }
 
+/* How many counter slots per PPE are carved out of the SRAM right now.  Zero
+ * until the NPU has agreed to provide counters, because a firmware that
+ * cannot count must not cost us half of the table: the carve-out is not free
+ * - it halves the entries every PPE can resolve - and paying it for counters
+ * that will never be read is a straight loss of offload capacity.
+ */
+static int airoha_ppe_get_num_stats_entries(struct airoha_ppe *ppe)
+{
+	int num_stats = airoha_ppe_stats_capable_entries(ppe);
+
+	if (num_stats > 0 && !ppe->foe_stats_enabled)
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+
+	return num_stats;
+}
+
 static int airoha_ppe_get_total_num_stats_entries(struct airoha_ppe *ppe)
 {
-	int num_stats = airoha_ppe_get_num_stats_entries(ppe);
+	int num_stats = airoha_ppe_stats_capable_entries(ppe);
 
 	if (num_stats > 0) {
 		struct airoha_eth *eth = ppe->eth;
@@ -209,7 +231,7 @@
 static void airoha_ppe_hw_init(struct airoha_ppe *ppe)
 {
 	u32 sram_ppe_num_data_entries = airoha_ppe_get_sram_num_entries(ppe);
-	u32 sram_num_entries;
+	u32 sram_num_entries, val;
 	u32 sram_tb_size, dram_num_entries;
 	struct airoha_eth *eth = ppe->eth;
 	int i, sram_num_stats_entries;
@@ -249,6 +271,7 @@
 			      PPE_SRAM_TABLE_EN_MASK |
 			      PPE_SRAM_HASH1_EN_MASK |
 			      PPE_DRAM_TABLE_EN_MASK |
+			      PPE_DRAM_HASH1_EN_MASK |
 			      PPE_SRAM_HASH0_MODE_MASK |
 			      PPE_SRAM_HASH1_MODE_MASK |
 			      PPE_DRAM_HASH0_MODE_MASK |
@@ -256,6 +279,8 @@
 			      FIELD_PREP(PPE_SRAM_TABLE_EN_MASK, 1) |
 			      FIELD_PREP(PPE_SRAM_HASH1_EN_MASK, 1) |
 			      FIELD_PREP(PPE_SRAM_HASH1_MODE_MASK, 1) |
+			      FIELD_PREP(PPE_DRAM_TABLE_EN_MASK, 1) |
+			      FIELD_PREP(PPE_DRAM_HASH1_EN_MASK, 1) |
 			      FIELD_PREP(PPE_DRAM_HASH1_MODE_MASK, 3));
 
 		airoha_fe_rmw(eth, REG_PPE_TB_CFG(i),
@@ -271,6 +296,20 @@
 			      FIELD_PREP(PPE_DRAM_TB_NUM_ENTRY_MASK,
 					 dram_num_entries));
 
+		/* The driver does not control this field, so make sure the
+		 * hardware accepted it.  The whole point of the DRAM half is
+		 * that fold_hash() spreads hashes across it; if the table is
+		 * off after all, those hashes are written to memory the PPE
+		 * never searches and the flows silently stay on the slow
+		 * path, which is exactly the state this patch is meant to
+		 * end.
+		 */
+		val = airoha_fe_rr(eth, REG_PPE_TB_HASH_CFG(i));
+		if (!(val & PPE_DRAM_TABLE_EN_MASK))
+			dev_warn(eth->dev,
+				 "PPE%u: DRAM table did not come up, %u entries unreachable\n",
+				 i + 1, PPE_DRAM_NUM_ENTRIES);
+
 		airoha_fe_rmw(eth, REG_PPE_BIND_RATE(i),
 			      PPE_BIND_RATE_L2B_BIND_MASK |
 			      PPE_BIND_RATE_BIND_MASK,
@@ -1092,12 +1131,27 @@
 				       u32 hash, bool rx_wlan)
 {
 	u32 sram_num_entries = airoha_ppe_get_total_sram_num_entries(ppe);
-	struct airoha_foe_entry *hwe = ppe->foe + hash * sizeof(*hwe);
+	struct airoha_foe_entry *hwe;
 	u32 ts = airoha_ppe_get_timestamp(ppe);
 	struct airoha_eth *eth = ppe->eth;
 	struct airoha_npu *npu;
 	int err = 0;
 
+	/* Every caller folds its hash through airoha_ppe_fold_hash() against
+	 * the same count, so this cannot trigger - but the check has to come
+	 * before the copy below, not after it, or a hash past the end of the
+	 * buffer would be written out of bounds and *then* rejected.
+	 */
+	if (hash >= airoha_ppe_get_total_num_entries(ppe))
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	hwe = ppe->foe + hash * sizeof(*hwe);
+
+	/* The SRAM half needs a control-register handshake, the DRAM half does
+	 * not: REG_PPE_TB_BASE() points straight at this buffer, so the copy
+	 * below *is* the write, and the barrier is what makes the payload
+	 * visible before the state field that claims it.
+	 */
 	memcpy(&hwe->d, &e->d, sizeof(*hwe) - sizeof(hwe->ib1));
 	wmb();
 
@@ -2093,6 +2147,13 @@
 	if (!ppe->foe_check_time)
 		return -ENOMEM;
 
+	/* Clear both PPE tables while the geometry is still the conservative
+	 * one.  foe_stats_enabled is false here, so the driver believes each
+	 * PPE has its full 8192 entries and therefore touches every slot the
+	 * hardware could ever resolve.  airoha_ppe_offload_setup() may later
+	 * shrink that to 4096 per PPE, but shrinking the range we already
+	 * cleared is safe; growing it after the fact would not be.
+	 */
 	err = airoha_ppe_flush_sram_entries(ppe);
 	if (err)
 		return err;
